    "lawCode": "Amd §2999-cc, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3293\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto expanding the originating sites at which telehealth services may be\ndelivered\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo authorize the delivery of telehealth services at any elementary or\nsecondary school, or child care program or center within the state.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends subdivision 3 of section 2999-cc of the public\nhealth law as amended by chapter 167 of the laws of 2015, by expanding\nthe meaning of originating site to include public, private and charter\nelementary and secondary schools, school age child care programs, and\nchild day care centers within the state of New York.\n\nSection two provides the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis legislation would expand the definition of originating site to\nallow primary care providers to use telehealth to connect with and\nprovide care to young children while they are in school or at a child\ncare setting where they spend so much of their time. Specifically,\nthese amendments would support existing models, such as the\nHealth-e-Access (HeA) Telemedicine model, which is supported by the\nStrong Pediatric Practice at the University of Rochester Medical\nCenter, as well as future models. The HeA model is an internet-based\npatient care approach that connects the patient to his/her primary\ncare medical home (PCMH). Telehealth models of care allow for services\nto reach patients where they are, even if there are not providers\navailable locally and also provides many other important benefits.\nThis legislation allows for children to access needed care from school\nwhere they spend most of their time through telehealth.\n\nChildren with telemedicine access from child care or elementary school\nmake 22% fewer emergency department (ED) visits than closely matched\ncounterparts. Based on a separate analysis, for children with severe\ndisabilities Who attend a child development center, ED visits dropped\n50% after HeA telemedicine services became available. Evidence also\nsuggests that HeA extends equity in access to impoverished, urban\nfamilies. This model was expanded in 2010 to extend HeA to older\nadults in senior living communities.\n\nThe issue schools are facing when they are short staffed is not the\nactual use of teletherapy, but whether or not reimburses for such\nservices using telemedicine is available when done in a school. When\nschools are unable to bill for such services it often makes the use of\nsuch service financially unfeasible. By adding schools to the list of\noriginating sites, schools will have a greater ability to consider\ntelehealth services when needed for their students.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe act shall take effect immediately.\n",
